---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Tag from '../components/Tag.astro';
---
<Base title="How we check" description="Where every station fact on EV Trip Reports comes from, how it is checked before a plan is delivered, and what the status labels mean.">
  <section class="band band--tight">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1 class="h1">How We Check</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:20px">One wrong stall count costs a driver an hour. So every station fact on this site has a source and the date we checked it, and anything we couldn't confirm says so.</p>

      <h2>Sources, in Order of Authority</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>The network's own locator.</strong> Tesla, Electrify America, EVgo, IONNA, Rivian Adventure Network, ChargePoint, bp pulse, Mercedes-Benz High-Power Charging, and Walmart. These decide stall count, maximum kW, who can use the site, and price.</li>
        <li><strong>The U.S. Department of Energy station database</strong> (Alternative Fuels Data Center). We use it to find every DC fast site in each stop town so nothing is missed. Where it disagrees with the network, the network wins and we show the disagreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Driver reports</strong> from Reddit, PlugShare, owner forums, and X. These tell us about broken stalls, waits, and new sites. Each one is quoted briefly, linked, and dated. A driver report stays a report. It never replaces a stall count, a kW figure, or an access flag from the network.</li>
        <li><strong>Third-party aggregators</strong> are leads only. We follow them back to a primary source and never cite them.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every station fact has a source link and a check date.</li>
        <li>We give kW and stall counts. We don't label sites "fast" or "slow."</li>
        <li>When sources disagree, we show both or mark the fact unverified.</li>
        <li>Nothing is filled in by guesswork. Unknowns say "Not yet verified."</li>
        <li>Every Tesla site is marked either Tesla only or open to all EVs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the Labels Mean</h2>
      <dl class="labels">
        <div><dt><Tag status="verified" /></dt><dd>Checked on the network's own locator on the date shown.</dd></div>
        <div><dt><Tag status="conflict" /></dt><dd>The network and the DOE database disagree. We show the network's number and note the other.</dd></div>
        <div><dt><Tag status="unverified" /></dt><dd>From the DOE database or a driver report only. We haven't confirmed it with the operator yet.</dd></div>
        <div><dt><Tag status="unpublished" /></dt><dd>The network doesn't publish this on its locator, usually price. Check its app.</dd></div>
        <div><dt><Tag status="tesla-only" /></dt><dd>Tesla vehicles only, per Tesla.</dd></div>
        <div><dt><Tag status="open" /></dt><dd>Tesla lists the site as open to other EVs. CCS cars need a NACS adapter.</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <h2>Before a Plan Reaches You</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Each stop is matched to your car's port, adapter, and charging memberships.</li>
        <li>Each stop is checked against the network's own locator, and the date of that check sits next to the fact.</li>
        <li>Anything we couldn't confirm is marked "Not yet verified" instead of left out.</li>
        <li>A person reviews the whole plan before it is sent.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A check date tells you what the network showed on that day. It doesn't tell you whether a charger is working right now, so check the network's app before you arrive. Prices change by time of day and by network plan, too.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .labels { margin: 16px 0 0; max-width: 68ch; }
  .labels div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr; gap: 16px; padding: 12px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); align-items: baseline; }
  .labels dd { margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .labels div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; } }
</style>
